body:json {
  {
    "content": "title: React Core Challenge\ndescription: Are you ready to truly grasp the fundamental building blocks of React? The **React Core Challenge** is your essential interactive companion for mastering the foundational concepts that power modern web development. This isn't just a quiz; it's a dynamic, engaging experience meticulously designed to solidify your understanding of **React.js essentials.**\n\nDive deep into core topics including component structure, state and props management, lifecycle methods (or hooks for functional components), JSX syntax, and basic event handling. Each question is carefully crafted to test your knowledge comprehensively, providing instant, insightful feedback to reinforce correct answers and clarify any misconceptions.\n\nPerfect for aspiring React developers taking their first steps, or seasoned coders looking to refresh their memory on core principles before tackling complex projects or technical interviews. Identify your knowledge gaps, transform theoretical concepts into practical understanding, and build a rock-solid foundation.\n\nStop wondering, start mastering! Take the React Core Challenge now and confidently elevate your React expertise!\n\n---question---\ntype: mcq\nquestion: What is the primary purpose of JSX in React?\noptions:\n1. To write CSS styles directly within JavaScript.\n2. To allow writing HTML-like syntax within JavaScript. [correct]\n3. To manage HTTP requests in React applications.\n4. To define state management logic for components.\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: trueFalse\nquestion: A React component can modify its own props directly.\ncorrect: false\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: written\nquestion: Explain the primary difference between a controlled component and an uncontrolled component in React forms.\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: multiSelect\nquestion: Which of the following statements about React Props are true?\noptions:\n1. Props are read-only. [correct]\n2. Props are used to pass data from a parent component to a child component. [correct]\n3. Props can be modified by the child component that receives them.\n4. Props are only accessible in class components.\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: mcq\nquestion: Which lifecycle method in a class component is typically used for fetching data from an API after the component mounts?\noptions:\n1. `componentWillMount()`\n2. `componentDidUpdate()`\n3. `componentDidMount()` [correct]\n4. `componentWillUnmount()`\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: trueFalse\nquestion: In functional components, the `useState` hook is used to add state to the component.\ncorrect: true\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: written\nquestion: Describe the purpose of the `key` prop when rendering a list of elements in React. Why is it important?\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: multiSelect\nquestion: Which of the following are valid ways to define a functional React component?\noptions:\n1. Using an arrow function. [correct]\n2. Using a regular JavaScript function declaration. [correct]\n3. Using the `class` keyword.\n4. Using an HTML element directly in the return.\ntags: React"
  }
}
